-- title:
--   Regular special fibre at an ordinary point of the ∞-branch
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q\ge 5$ and $M'\ge 1$ with $q\nmid M'$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero, algebraic over $\mathbb Q$, containing a primitive $q$-th root of unity $\zeta$ and admitting a ring homomorphism to $\mathbb C$ carrying $\zeta$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$. Let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L\subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ obtained as `laurentBaseChange`, i.e. generated over $L$ by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field `xHFunctionField` at level $q^2M'$ for the subgroup $H=\ker\big((\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q)^\times\big)$, and let $A$ be a discrete valuation ring with fraction field $L$, with $q$ in its maximal ideal, with $\zeta$ in its image in $L$, with uniformiser $\varpi$ generating the maximal ideal, and with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with that on $L$. Let $j\in K$ be a nonzero element whose Laurent expansion is the $q$-expansion `jq` of the modular invariant, and let $W_0$ be a valuation subring of $K$ characterised by: $f\in W_0$ iff $f\cdot y=x$ in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ for some power series $x,y$ over $A$ with $y$ of nonzero reduction modulo the maximal ideal of $A$. Let $\mathfrak X=$ `TwoChartIntegralModel` $A$ $K$ $j$ be the pushout of the two charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $j$- and $j^{-1}$-chart algebras (the elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$, resp. $A[j^{-1}]$), and $z$ a point of $\mathfrak X$ such that the germ $\varpi_z$ at $z$ of the global function coming from $\varpi$ under $\mathfrak X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk. Assume $z$ is the image under the chart immersion of a point $y$ of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $j$-finite chart algebra whose prime is maximal; that there are an algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ with decidable equality and a ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the $j$-finite chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel $y$ such that $\varphi(j)$ does not lie in `ssJSet` $q$ $\Omega$ (the set of $j$-values all of whose elliptic models have trivial $q$-torsion); and that every element of the chart algebra lying in the nonunits of $W_0$ lies in $y$. Let $K_0\le K$ be the analogous $L$-compositum of the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_0(M')$, again an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$, with a nonzero $j_0\in K_0$ of the same Laurent expansion, with a ring homomorphism $\iota$ between the two $j$-finite chart algebras induced by the inclusion $K_0\subseteq K$, and with the structure morphism of the $K_0$-model to $\operatorname{Spec}A$ smooth. Let $\mathfrak Q_0$ be a prime of the $K_0$-chart algebra contained in $\iota^{-1}(y)$ and not containing $\varpi$, such that in every localisation $O$ of that algebra at $\iota^{-1}(y)$ the extension of $\mathfrak Q_0$ together with $\varpi$ generates the maximal ideal of $O$. Then, regarding the $K$-chart algebra as an algebra over the $K_0$-chart algebra via $\iota$, the following implication holds: if the extension is unramified at every height-one prime $\mathfrak Q\le y$ with $\iota^{-1}(\mathfrak Q)\neq\mathfrak Q_0$, and if the residue field extension of the $K$-chart algebra modulo $y$ over the $K_0$-chart algebra modulo $\iota^{-1}(y)$ is separable, then the quotient of the stalk of $\mathfrak X$ at $z$ by the ideal generated by $\varpi_z$ is a regular local ring.
--
--   This is Abhyankar's lemma in the modular setting: a cover of the smooth $\Gamma_0(M')$-floor which is unramified in codimension one away from one section transversal to the special fibre, and tamely ramified along it, has regular special fibre at the point in question; the ordinariness condition on the $j$-value and the condition of lying on the $\infty$-branch of the Gauss valuation ring are what supply tameness and the local shape of the branch locus. It feeds the regularity statement for the special fibre of the two-chart integral model of $X_H(q^2M')$ at ordinary points of the $\infty$-branch over a perfect residue field, used in the study of the reduction of the modular curves occurring in the Frey–Serre–Ribet argument.
